The interplay between on-board storage constraints and payload power allocation in satellite communication systems is a critical aspect that significantly impacts system performance and efficiency. On-board storage constraints, particularly in low-orbit (LOO) and medium-orbit (MEO) satellites, are limited by their on-board memory capacities, which can store data up to 100 KB for LOO satellites and up to 100 MB for MEO satellites. These constraints necessitate efficient power allocation strategies to ensure that data is transmitted promptly without exceeding memory limits. The impact of these constraints extends to time-tagged services, which require additional memory space for processing after the satellite is ready.

The impact on latency and throughput is significant. Time-tagged services, which require additional memory space, often face increased latency due to the need for data storage and retrieval. This can lead to higher latency and reduced throughput. Additionally, the power allocation for time-tagged services must be optimized to balance processing time with memory usage, ensuring that the system remains efficient and responsive.

To address these challenges, advanced power allocation techniques are essential. Adaptive power control algorithms can dynamically adjust power consumption based on the current state of the system, optimizing resource usage while meeting QoS requirements. Machine learning and AI-based optimization can further enhance power allocation by learning from historical data and adapting to changing conditions, ensuring that power is allocated efficiently and effectively.

Future research directions include exploring the integration of machine learning and AI to dynamically adjust power allocation, leveraging AI for adaptive power control, and investigating energy harvesting technologies to reduce reliance on traditional power sources. These advancements aim to develop intelligent and efficient power allocation strategies that optimize both immediate and time-tagged service operations while effectively managing on-board storage constraints.

