### Introduction and Discussion

This paper explores the use of a hybrid SIW-waveguide feeding topology for V-band satellite antenna designs. While this topology offers advantages in terms of efficiency and compactness, the authors highlight a trade-off between performance and size. Specifically, they observe a decrease in efficiency as array scale increases, primarily due to increased insertion loss within the SIW structure.

This trade-off raises the question of how to balance efficiency and compactness in larger antenna arrays. The authors propose that a key solution lies in optimizing the transition between SIW and waveguide structures. By carefully designing the transition, they aim to maintain high efficiency while reducing the overall size of the antenna.

The proposed transition involves a vertical transition with wide bandwidth and compact configuration. This design is shown to offer a balance between high efficiency and compactness, effectively addressing the challenges encountered in larger arrays. The optimized dimensions of the proposed transition are highlighted, ensuring that the antenna maintains high efficiency even as the array scale increases.

By focusing on this transition, the authors aim to provide a practical solution for designing V-band satellite antennas that can meet both performance and compactness requirements. This approach not only addresses the limitations of traditional SIW and waveguide designs but also opens up new possibilities for further optimization in future designs.

